# JDK vs JRE vs JVM

JDK (Java Development Kit) is a software development kit used to develop Java applications. It includes the Java Runtime Environment (JRE), a compiler (javac), debugger, and other tools needed for Java development.

JRE (Java Runtime Environment) is a part of the JDK that provides the libraries, Java Virtual Machine (JVM), and other components to run Java applications.

JVM (Java Virtual Machine) runs Java bytecode and makes Java platform-independent.

In summary, JDK is for development, JRE is for running Java applications, and JVM is the engine that executes Java bytecode.

